---
.title = "Type conversions",
.date = .date("2026-09-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "doc.shtml",
.translation_key = "reference/types/conversions",
.custom = { "section": "Types and values" },
---

Choose among three operations:

- `as(kind)` checks the current zval type and reads it without conversion.
- `cast(kind)` returns the result of a PHP explicit cast and preserves the source zval.
- `convert(kind)` applies a PHP explicit cast in place.

Both `cast` and `convert` support `.int`, `.float`, `.bool`, `.str`, `.array` and `.object`. `cast(.str)` returns an owned Zend string. `convert(.str)` borrows the resulting Zend string and `convert(.string)` borrows its bytes. `cast` does not support `.string`.

| Operation | Effect on source | Result ownership |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `as(kind)` | Checks the type only | Copies scalars and borrows slices or wrappers |
| `cast(kind)` | Preserves the value | Caller owns `.str`, `.array` and `.object` results |
| `convert(kind)` | Replaces the value | Borrows from the converted zval for pointer and slice results |

# [Casting without modifying the source]($heading.id('preserve-source'))

```zig
const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{.{ .mixed = .{} }}, {});
const text = try args[0].cast(.str);
defer text.release();
ctx.ret(.string, text.slice());
```

`cast(.str)` returns a `*zend.String`. Borrow its bytes with `slice()`. Release or transfer converted strings, arrays and objects. Scalars need no release. `cast` preserves the source zval, but arrays and objects may still share underlying data.

# [Convert in place]($heading.id('convert-in-place'))

```zig
const args = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{.{ .mixed = .{} }}, {});
phpz.Zval.raw.copy(ctx.retval.ptr(), args[0].ptr());
_ = try ctx.retval.convert(.int);
```

Copy the input into the zval at `ctx.retval` before converting the copy in place. `convert` unwraps the reference and leaves other reference owners with their original value. A borrowed slice or object pointer is invalidated when the converted zval changes or is released.

After a failed `convert`, the source may already have changed. A failed `cast` preserves it. Both operations can invoke object handlers and raise PHP exceptions. Only convert actual PHP values. Internal states such as `undef` and `indirect` are unsuitable.

# [Target types]($heading.id('target-types'))

| Target | `cast` result | `convert` result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `.int` | `i64` | `i64` |
| `.float` | `f64` | `f64` |
| `.bool` | `bool` | `bool` |
| `.str` | Owned `*zend.String` | Borrowed `*zend.String` |
| `.string` | Unsupported | Borrowed `[]const u8` |
| `.array` | Owned `*zend.Array` | Borrowed `*zend.Array` |
| `.object` | Owned `*zend.Object` | Borrowed `*zend.Object` |
